12741:82:􀢄  Test readmeAndCurrentStateUseTheSameEndpointBoundary() recorded an issue at TunnelGuidanceTests.swift:24:5: Expectation failed: (normalizedWhitespace(currentState) → "# Current state and future VPN boundary Last verified: 2026-07-28. ## What exists The repository builds one SwiftPM macOS 14 executable, `ReluxProxy`, packaged by shell scripts as a menu bar `.app` and DMG. The app: - stores an ordered collection of profiles as one schema-versioned JSON Data value under `legacySocksProfiles.v1`, with stable UUID identity and unique explicit local ports; - migrates the legacy `sshHost`, `sshAccount`, and `localPort` values exactly once, leaves those keys untouched for rollback, and never resurrects deleted profiles after the migration marker is set; - owns one independent runtime session per profile, allowing multiple SSH children and SOCKS tunnels to connect concurrently on different local ports; - launches `/usr/bin/ssh -N -C -D 127.0.0.1:<port>` with fail-fast, keepalive, timeout, batch-mode, and quiet logging options; - passes an empty account as the bare host target, allowing system OpenSSH to use a matching `User` setting (or its normal default); a non-empty account is sent with `-l account` while the destination remains the bare host or alias, which explicitly overrides only the SSH user; - accepts an IP address, hostname, or `Host` alias and lets the system client apply matching `~/.ssh/config` behavior such as `HostName`, `Port`, `IdentityFile`, and `ProxyJump`; - binds the SOCKS listener only to IPv4 loopback (`127.0.0.1`) and keeps a separate trailing 4,000-character in-memory diagnostic for every session; - rejects duplicate saved ports and externally occupied loopback ports without stopping or modifying another session or process; - reports connecting, connected, disconnecting, disconnected, and failed states per row, with aggregate menu-bar status; - prevents profile edits and removal while that profile owns a starting, connected, or stopping child; - stops every child it owns on normal quit and starts every saved profile disconnected on the next launch, with no automatic retry or reconnect; - exposes every listed endpoint as an Application SOCKS5 proxy — TCP only; not a system VPN or system-wide routing; users configure a SOCKS-aware client manually and enable proxy DNS/SOCKS5h when private names need the remote resolver; - intentionally runs outside the App Sandbox so it can spawn system SSH. Tests cover profile migration and persistence, command construction and account precedence, duplicate and OS port conflicts, concurrent independent lifecycle, stale callback protection, diagnostics, active mutation rejection, and coordinated shutdown. CI runs `swift test` and builds an ad-hoc-signed universal application bundle. Tagged releases import the Relux Works Developer ID certificate, build a universal app, sign the DMG, notarize and staple it, validate it with Gatekeeper, publish checksums/provenance, and upload both versioned and stable `ReluxProxy.dmg` assets. ## What does not exist The current app is not a system VPN and does not contain: - iOS targets; - `NETunnelProviderManager` or packet-tunnel extensions; - a userspace TCP/IP stack or packetFlow bridge; - an embedded SSH implementation; - UDP or system DNS forwarding; - the remote `relux-relay` executable/protocol; - shared profile/Keychain state between host and extension; - Network Extension entitlements or provisioning; - TestFlight/App Store release automation. ## Implemented legacy migration The [multi-profile usage guide](../README.md#use) describes the user-facing workflow. The implementation above provides the one-time migration, profile persistence, OpenSSH account precedence, port ownership, diagnostics, and independent lifecycle. Runtime state and reconnect intent are never persisted. The [remote VPN reachability guide](socks-vpn-reachability.md) documents the same application-level boundary, proxy-side DNS, and redacted diagnostics. A remote VPN route, the authenticated `sshd` forwarding process/account policy, and the remote firewall must all permit the requested TCP destination; Relux Proxy does not change local system proxy, DNS, or routes. The planned VPN product in `.spec/` remains a new architecture, not an incremental toggle inside the legacy SOCKS controller. For future VPN work: - the stable macOS release path must remain reproducible; - any migration from legacy SOCKS profiles to VPN profiles still needs its own explicit product decision; - the system-SSH implementation may remain as a macOS legacy mode, be replaced, or be retired only through a product decision; - SwiftPM tests and release history must not be silently discarded; - Network Extension code must not be backported into the current manual app without the architecture and entitlement gates in `.spec/validation.md`. ").contains(TunnelGuidance.endpointLabel → "Application SOCKS5 proxy — TCP only; not a system VPN or system-wide routing.")
